fix(signing): commit the paths the caller named, not the shared index (#280)

Two writers shared one working tree. One staged a file and was refused by
staged_ref, correctly -- the index had moved under it. Seconds later the other
committed, and its commit carried three files: its own two, and the one the
first writer had staged. Nothing was lost; what landed was worse, a signed and
permanent record of work its message and its identity did not name.

sign_commit commits THE INDEX, and the index belongs to the working tree rather
than to the caller. staged_ref catches the index moving UNDER a signer. Nothing
caught a commit sweeping up what another writer had staged.

One rule, both paths, and it is #261's: everything that would be COMMITTED must
be a subset of what the caller can account for, and a caller that cannot account
for a path is refused naming it -- the whole commit, never narrowed, because
narrowing means unstaging work that belongs to somebody else. Only the source of
the accounting differs. A stopped run cannot be asked anything, so a capture
reads its tool-call bodies; a caller that is still running says, with -p.

Serialising was the other candidate and cannot work. The interleaving happened
entirely outside the commit path: the sweeping writer's `git add` landed before
its commit ever started, so no lock the script takes reaches back over it, and a
lock binds only the parties that take it while the other party is a bare
`git add` in a shell. Separate indexes fail twice over -- every writer would have
to opt in, and the commit is made by a server process that reads .git/index
whatever the script sets. Naming is the only account of "mine" that survives
contact with a second writer.

  sign_commit  gains an optional `paths`, omitempty like `worktree`, and refuses
               before Phase A -- after staged_ref, which stays the first gate --
               when the index holds anything it does not name.
  the script   gains -p, refuses a caller committing its own work that named
               nothing, and accounts before it stages as well as after, so a
               refusal leaves every writer's work exactly where it was.
  -r           is exempt: the harness capture path is already bounded by #261
               and is the one caller that cannot be asked to say so, because the
               run whose work it is has already gone.

A deployment that predates the argument answers `unexpected additional
properties ["paths"]` and would otherwise stop a human from committing at all,
so the call drops to a second attempt without it and says which half of the
bound is missing -- the ladder register_run already has, for the same reason.

Driven by TestAnIndexHoldingAPathTheCallerDidNotNameIsRefusedNamingIt and
TestACommitSignsOnlyThePathsItsCallerNamed, both observed failing with "the call
succeeded; it had to be refused"; and by the commit selftest's two-writer cases,
observed against the previous script signing a tree carrying both writers' files.

